멕시코와 미래 5년 포괄적 협력 청사진 마련
'2026-2030 공동행동계획' 합의



| 스마트비즈 = 이장혁 기자 | 대한민국 정부가 멕시코와의 전략적 동반자 관계를 전방위로 심화하는 내용을 담은 '2026-2030 한-멕시코 공동행동계획'에 합의했다.
이재명 대통령의 멕시코 국빈 방문을 계기로 지난 24일 클라우디아 셰인바움 파르도 멕시코 대통령과의 정상회담에서 도출된 결실로, 양국 간 오랜 우정을 바탕으로 미래 번영을 향한 제도적 도약대를 마련했다는 평가다.
양국은 고위급 외교 채널과 다자 무대에서의 정책 조율을 정례화하며 외교적 결속을 공고히 다진다.
외교부 차관급 고위 정책협의회를 정기적으로 개최하고, 2027년 한국에서 열리는 회의를 기점으로 차관보급 '경제·과학·기술협력 공동위원회'를 매년 가동하기로 합의했다. 국제무대에서는 중견국 협의체인 믹타(MIKTA) 각급 회의 참여를 확대하고, 멕시코가 의장국을 맡는 2028년 아시아태평양경제협력체(APEC)의 인공지능(AI) 이니셔티브 이행을 적극 지원한다. 2028년 한국과 2029년 멕시코의 주요 20개국(G20) 의장국 수임 추진에 맞춰 글로벌 경제 현안 조율에도 긴밀히 협력할 방침이다.
무역과 투자, 금융 인프라 확충을 위한 안전판도 대폭 강화됐다. 양국 정부는 '투자의 증진 및 상호보호를 위한 협정' 개정 협상을 성공적으로 매듭짓고 후속 절차를 차질 없이 밟기로 했다.
한국수출입은행과 멕시코 대외무역은행 간에 최대 1억 달러 규모의 은행 간 전대금융 개설을 협의해 현지 진출 기업의 금융 부담을 던다. 2027년 하반기 '한-멕시코 국제개발협력포럼'을 신설해 중남미 개발 원조 프로젝트를 발굴하며, 관세청 간 위험정보 교환과 지식재산처의 AI 특허 심사 기준 공유를 통해 통관 안전성과 지식재산권 보호 환경을 선진화한다.
미래 신산업을 선도할 AI와 우주 항공 분야의 파트너십 역시 본궤도에 오른다.
행정안전부와 멕시코 디지털전환통신청이 디지털정부 모범 사례와 기술 지원 교류를 추진하고, 한국 우주항공청과 멕시코 우주청은 평화적 목적의 우주 탐사 및 산업 육성을 위한 양해각서(MOU)를 맺는다. 2026년 하반기에는 양국 공동으로 '한-멕시코 우주의 날'을 열어 민간 우주 생태계 간 교류의 장을 열고, 기획재정부의 지식공유프로그램(KSP)을 연계해 중남미 우주청(ALCE) 회원국의 정책 수립을 지원할 계획이다.
사회 전반의 교류와 안전망 구축을 위한 실무 협력도 병행된다.
문화유산 공동연구 심포지엄과 신규 정부 장학사업 실무작업반을 가동해 학술 및 인적 교류를 넓히고, 미래 세대를 위한 반부패 교육 방법론을 나눈다. 농수축산 분야에서는 농촌진흥청의 기술 교류와 수산물 위생 증명 및 축산물 전자증명 협력을 확대하며 해기사 면허 상호 인정을 추진한다. 경찰청과 국민권익위원회는 멕시코 치안·행정 당국과 공조해 초국가범죄 대응 및 부패 사전 경보 시스템을 공유함으로써 양국 사회의 공공 신뢰를 높여나갈 방침이다.
양국은 이번 공동행동계획을 통해 다방면의 실천적 과제들을 흔들림 없이 이행하며 태평양을 잇는 지속가능한 미래 상생 파트너십을 완성해 나갈 방침이다.
On the occasion of the State Visit of H.E. Lee Jae Myung, President of the Republic of Korea, to the United Mexican States, President Lee and H.E. Claudia Sheinbaum Pardo, President of the United Mexican States, held a summit meeting on September 24th 2026 and agreed as follows:
Cooperation Mechanisms
a. Promote regular high-level exchanges, including high-level meetings on the margins of multilateral events;
b. Hold regular meetings of the bilateral High-Level Policy Consultation at the vice-minister of Foreign Affairs level;
c. Resume meetings of the Joint Committee on Economic, Scientific and Technical Cooperation and convene them annually at the deputy-minister of Foreign Affairs level, beginning in 2027 in the Republic of Korea;
d. Further enhance cooperation in addressing global challenges and strengthening multilateralism, including through active participation in MIKTA at all levels, from Senior Officials’ Meetings and Foreign Ministers’ Meetings to Leaders’ Gatherings;
e. Strengthen cooperation within the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC), including by supporting the implementation of the APEC Artificial Intelligence Initiative through its mid-term review in 2028 during Mexico’s APEC host year;
f. Strengthen coordination and cooperation within the G20 to ensure that the G20 continues to evolve as the premier forum for international economic cooperation, with a view to Korea’s Presidency in 2028 and Mexico’s bid for the G20 Presidency in 2029.
Trade, Finance, Investment and Development Cooperation
a. Further strengthen the bilateral economic relationship based on mutual benefit, with a particular focus on enhancing cooperation and promoting investment between the two countries;
b. Welcome the successful conclusion of negotiations and work toward finalizing and signing the modernized Agreement between the Government of the Republic of Korea and the Government of the United Mexican States for the Promotion and Reciprocal Protection of Investments to strengthen the institutional foundations for investment;
c. Strengthen financial cooperation, including entering into discussions regarding the establishment of an Interbank Credit Facility in the amount up to USD 100 million between the Export-Import Bank of Korea and Banco Nacional de Comercio Exterior;
d. Establish the Korea-Mexico Forum on International Development Cooperation as a high-level dialogue mechanism, with a view to discussing shared priorities in each side’s development cooperation policies and exploring potential avenues for strategic and field-level collaboration in Latin America and the Caribbean in the second half of 2027;
e. Discuss practical arrangements for IP data exchange between the Ministry of Intellectual Property of Korea (MOIP) and the Mexican Institute of Industrial Property (IMPI);
f. Continue bilateral efforts to help distinguish between counterfeit and authentic goods, including by organizing seminars between MOIP’s IP-Center and IMPI;
g. Promote the exchange of risk information on high-risk cargo and the sharing of knowledge and experience in AI-based customs administration, including through the establishment of a working group between the Korea Customs Service and the National Customs Agency (ANAM) of Mexico;
h. Strengthen bilateral cooperation in water-related matters, through closer engagement between AMEXCID and K-water, to promote knowledge-sharing, capacity-building and the exploration of potential joint projects in areas of mutual interest.
AI and Space
a. Pursue cooperation in the field of AI and digital government through activities such as the sharing of best practices, the exchange of experts, policy consultations, and technical assistance between the Ministry of the Interior and Safety of Korea and the Agency for Digital Transformation and Telecommunications (ATDT) of Mexico;
b. Explore ways to exchange information between MOIP and IMPI on examination criteria for AI-related inventions;
c. Pursue space cooperation for peaceful purposes, including through the signing of a Memorandum of Understanding between the Korea AeroSpace Administration (KASA), ATDT, and the Mexican Space Agency (AEM) to identify and develop cooperative projects;
d. Promote networking and mutual understanding between the space industries of the two countries, with a view to exploring potential cooperation, including through the organization of a Korea-Mexico Space Day in the second half of 2026, to be jointly convened by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Korea, KASA, and ATDT;
e. Cooperate to support the member states of the Latin American and Caribbean Space Agency (ALCE) in developing space policies and industrial strategies, including through the launch of a project under the Knowledge Sharing Program between the Ministry of Finance and Economy of Korea and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Mexico;
Culture and Education
a. Promote ongoing cooperation in the fields of cultural heritage, creative industries and the arts, including through the establishment of a working group for information exchange, capacity-building initiatives, the promotion of co-production and co-creation, and the use of cultural spaces for hosting events, with the aim of fostering regular exchanges in the fields of culture and the arts between the two countries;
b. Establish a cooperative mechanism for the preservation and management of cultural heritage, with a particular focus on the survey, documentation, conservation, and sustainable management of archaeological heritage affected by large-scale infrastructure
and development projects, as well as expert exchanges and joint research, beginning with a bilateral symposium in 2027;
c. Encourage student exchanges between the two countries, including through the establishment of a joint working group between the Ministry of Education of Korea and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Mexico in the second half of 2026 to explore the possibility of creating of a new scholarship program;
d. Promote academic exchanges between the two countries, including by encouraging the resumption of the Korea-Mexico University Presidents’ Forum between the Korean Council for University Education and the National Association of Universities and Higher Education Institutions (ANUIES);
e. Promote cooperation on integrity and anti-corruption education for children and youth, including through the exchange of methodologies, educational materials, experiential learning tools, teacher training, youth participation initiatives, and joint activities aimed at strengthening a culture of integrity, civic responsibility, and prevention of corruption from an early age;
f. Encourage cooperation on citizen engagement and public awareness of anti-corruption efforts, including the exchange of methodologies for measuring corruption and integrity perceptions, communicating policy results in an accessible manner, strengthening citizen participation, and developing evidence-based initiatives aimed at increasing public trust in anti-corruption institutions.
Agriculture, Fishery and Livestock
a. Strengthen cooperation in agricultural technology and industry to address common challenges facing the two countries, including through the establishment of a working group between the Rural Development Administration of Korea (RDA) and the National Institute for Forestry, Agriculture and Livestock Research of Mexico (INIFAP);
b. Cooperate to ensure the safety of Mexican fishery products exported to Korea and facilitate the export process, including the associated registration and health certification procedures administered by the Federal Commission for the Protection against Sanitary Risks (COFEPRIS) on the Mexican side;
c. Promote cooperation on electronic certification for livestock products between the Ministry of Food and Drug Safety of Korea and the Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Development of Mexico (SADER);
d. Strengthen cooperation on maritime affairs, including through the mutual recognition of seafarers’ training and certificates of competency issued by the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries of Korea and the Secretariat of the Navy of Mexico.
Defense, Security and Anti-corruption
a. Expand defense cooperation through policy dialogue, personnel exchanges, research and development, and defense industrial cooperation;
b. Establish a strategic mechanism between the Korean National Police Agency and the
Ministry of Security and Citizen Protection of Mexico (SSPC) for comprehensive cooperation on peace and security, information sharing, and the exchange of best practices in combating transnational crime;
c. Strengthen institutional cooperation between the Ministry of Anti-Corruption and Good Governance of Mexico and the Anti-Corruption and Civil Rights Commission of Korea through the exchange of policies, methodologies, non-confidential information, and best practices, and develop joint programs on corruption prevention, integrity, education, professionalization, citizen participation, and institutional capacity-building;
d. Promote technical cooperation on corruption risk prevention and integrity management, including methodologies for risk mapping, early-warning systems, conflict-of-interest prevention, integrity assessments, data analytics, and mechanisms to identify and address corruption-prone areas before irregularities occur.
